# modules/image/checks-dpkg.rules - the container-image module's dpkg-
# specific script-check registry (rules/RULE-FORMAT.md §9's
# `checks-<name>.rules` row, docs/FOUNDATION.md tension 29).
#
# ONE FILE PER OWNING DRIVER, per the "one registry per owner" binding rule (the
# cloud-wave `checks.rules` corruption, `c9f0e80`, is the incident that rule
# exists to prevent) and `modules/image/checks-apk.rules`' own identical
# precedent: this ticket's OWN file, never an append to `checks-apk.rules` or
# to a shared `modules/image/checks.rules` a later rpm ticket could then
# collide on.
#
# `id: IMAGE-PKG-VULNERABLE_OS_PACKAGE-02` IS DPKG-SPECIFIC, despite its
# generic-sounding name - the identical convention `checks-apk.rules`' own
# `-01` id already established ("IS APK-SPECIFIC, despite its
# generic-sounding name"). A check id names one owning file under §9.1.1's
# uniqueness rule (E019), so `IMAGE-PKG-VULNERABLE_OS_PACKAGE-01` (apk,
# `checks-apk.rules`) stays untouched and this ticket adds its own `-02`
# sibling in its own file, exactly as `checks-apk.rules`' own header
# predicted ("so IMG-07's dpkg ticket adds its OWN id in its OWN
# checks-dpkg.rules"). A future rpm ticket (IMG-12) adds a third sibling,
# `-03`, in its own `checks-rpm.rules`.
#
# `script: distro/dpkg.sh` because a future dpkg comparator (IMG-08) and the
# finding emission a future Debian/Ubuntu advisory-ecosystem ticket (IMG-09)
# wires are both expected to land in that same file, mirroring
# `checks-apk.rules`' own `script: distro/apk.sh` before IMG-05/IMG-06 landed
# on top of it.
#
# THIS CHECK DOES NOT FIRE YET (IMG-07's own scope line: "dpkg enumeration -
# Status gate + Source: fallback, own checks-dpkg.rules; no comparator, no
# advisories, no finding yet"). `distro/dpkg.sh` ships an enumerator and no
# comparator, and nothing in `modules/image/run.sh` calls it -
# `var/lib/dpkg/status` is not even in that file's own explicit wanted-path
# list yet ("var/lib/dpkg/status stays
# OFF this list - dpkg parsing is IMG-07's scope, not this ticket's"). This
# is the identical "registered, not yet reachable" shape
# `modules/image/checks-apk.rules` itself was in from IMG-04 through IMG-05,
# and the `modules/sast/`/`modules/dast/` precedent this whole module
# follows. `checks_registry_load` globs every `*.rules` file with no
# reachability requirement, so a check id registered here today costs
# nothing and is already correct the day a future dpkg comparator/matcher
# ticket lands.
#
# `severity: high` is the REGISTERED DEFAULT, not a promise every finding
# will carry it - mirroring `checks-apk.rules`' own identical comment: the
# real severity is expected to come from `data/advisories.db`'s own
# per-advisory `severity` field once a matcher exists, the same
# `finding_set base_severity "$sev"` convention `modules/network/
# checks-banner.rules`' NET-SVC-OUTDATED_COMPONENT-01 and
# `checks-apk.rules`' own `-01` id both already apply.
#
# `tags: passive` - dpkg enumeration reads one already-extracted local file
# and makes no network call and mutates nothing, the identical reasoning
# `checks-apk.rules`' own header gives for the same tag. `tags: quick` for
# the identical reason that file gives too: a single-file parse is the
# cheapest real coverage a run can buy.
#
# `coverage-scope: image-id` - the only legal value for the `IMAGE` module
# (rules/RULE-FORMAT.md §9.5.1's frozen table); the cell is the
# operator-declared stable id from `config/images.conf`, never the image
# digest or tag - a rebuild or retag must not reset coverage.

id: IMAGE-PKG-VULNERABLE_OS_PACKAGE-02
title: Installed dpkg package matches a known advisory for this image's Debian/Ubuntu release
script: distro/dpkg.sh
severity: high
confidence: high
cwe: CWE-1104
owasp: A06:2021
tags: passive
tags: quick
coverage-scope: image-id
remediation: Rebuild the image against an updated base layer (or upgrade
  the named package directly, where the Dockerfile installs it explicitly)
  so the installed version no longer matches this advisory, then re-scan.
  A base-image bump alone can carry this fix silently, so treat a `FROM`
  tag pin as something to review whenever this finding's advisory has a
  fixed-in version at or below the base image's own release.
references: CWE-1104
references: https://owasp.org/Top10/A06_2021-Vulnerable_and_Outdated_Components/
